// Blog posts batch 1 (2026-10-16): beef salads and curries. Rules in posts-blog.js.
import { BLOG_KEYWORDS } from './posts-blog-keywords.js'

const meta = (slug) => ({ slug, kw: BLOG_KEYWORDS[slug].focus, keywords: BLOG_KEYWORDS[slug].keywords, date: '2026-10-16', updated: '2026-10-16' })

export const BLOG_POSTS_1 = [
  {
    ...meta('thai-beef-salad'),
    title: 'Thai Beef Salad: Recipe, Dressing and the Best Cut to Use',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Thai Beef Salad Recipe and Dressing',
    excerpt: `A Thai beef salad with a sharp lime and chilli dressing, the best halal cuts to grill for it, and how to slice the beef so every bite stays tender.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `A good Thai beef salad is all about contrast: hot, smoky slices of charred beef tossed through cold, crunchy vegetables, fresh herbs and a dressing that is sour, salty, sweet and hot all at once. In Thailand the dish is called yum nua, and it is one of the best ways we know to turn a single steak into a meal for four. It is quick, it is light, and it works just as well for a weeknight dinner as it does on a share table with friends. This guide covers a reliable Thai beef salad recipe, the dressing, which cut to buy and the small techniques that make the difference.`],
      ['h2', `What makes a great Thai beef salad`],
      ['p', `Three things decide whether the salad is memorable or merely fine. The first is the beef: it needs a good crust from a very hot pan or grill, a pink centre, and a proper rest before slicing. The second is the dressing, which should taste slightly too strong on its own, because the beef, herbs and vegetables will soften it once everything is tossed. The third is texture. Toasted rice powder, crushed peanuts or fried shallots add crunch, and plenty of fresh mint and coriander keep the salad lively.`],
      ['p', `Everything else is flexible. Cucumber, cherry tomatoes, red onion or shallots, bean sprouts, snake beans and crisp lettuce all belong here. Use what is in the fridge, but keep the herbs generous: they are an ingredient in this dish, not a garnish.`],
      ['h2', `The best beef for a Thai beef salad`],
      ['p', `You want a steak with real beef flavour that slices neatly when cooked medium-rare. Our first pick is [rump steak](/product/rump-beef-steaks/): it is flavoursome, holds its shape and is good value for a dish where the beef is sliced thinly anyway. [Sirloin porterhouse](/product/sirloin-steak-beef-porterhouse/) is a step up in tenderness, and [flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/) gives the most traditional result, with a coarse grain that soaks up the dressing.`],
      ['p', `Beef skirt meat is another classic choice in Thai and Vietnamese kitchens. It is thin, loose-grained and full of flavour, and it cooks in minutes. Whatever you choose, look for a piece about 2.5 to 3 cm thick so you can build a dark crust without overcooking the centre. Lean cuts such as eye fillet work, but they lack the flavour to stand up to a punchy dressing.`],
      ['h3', `How much beef per person`],
      ['p', `Allow about 120 to 150 g of raw beef per person for a main course, or 80 to 100 g if the salad is one of several shared dishes. A 600 g rump comfortably feeds four with rice on the side.`],
      ['h2', `Thai beef salad recipe`],
      ['p', `This Thai beef salad recipe serves four as a main. Take the steak out of the fridge 30 minutes before cooking, and have the vegetables and dressing ready before the beef hits the pan, because the salad should be tossed while the beef is still warm.`],
      ['ul', [
        `600 g rump, porterhouse or flank steak`,
        `1 tablespoon oil, plus salt and cracked black pepper`,
        `1 Lebanese cucumber, halved and sliced`,
        `200 g cherry tomatoes, halved`,
        `2 red shallots or half a small red onion, finely sliced`,
        `1 large handful each of mint, coriander and Thai basil leaves`,
        `1 cup bean sprouts and a few torn lettuce leaves`,
        `2 tablespoons toasted rice powder or crushed roasted peanuts`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Pat the steak dry, rub it with oil and season well. Heat a heavy pan, chargrill or barbecue until very hot and cook the steak for about 3 to 4 minutes a side for medium-rare, depending on thickness. Rest it on a board for at least 8 minutes. While it rests, toss the vegetables and herbs in a large bowl. Slice the beef thinly across the grain, add it to the bowl with any resting juices, pour over the dressing and toss gently. Scatter with the toasted rice powder or peanuts and serve straight away.`],
      ['h2', `Thai beef salad dressing`],
      ['p', `The Thai beef salad dressing is where the dish comes alive. Mix it in a jar and taste it before you dress the salad: it should hit all four notes clearly.`],
      ['ul', [
        `3 tablespoons fresh lime juice`,
        `2 tablespoons fish sauce`,
        `1 tablespoon grated palm sugar or brown sugar`,
        `1 to 2 bird's eye chillies, finely chopped`,
        `1 small clove garlic, crushed`,
        `1 teaspoon light soy sauce (optional, for depth)`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Stir until the sugar dissolves. If it tastes too sharp, add a little more sugar; too sweet, a squeeze more lime; flat, a splash more fish sauce. For a milder version for children, leave the chilli out of the dressing and serve sliced chilli on the side.`],
      ['h3', `Making toasted rice powder`],
      ['p', `Dry-fry 2 tablespoons of uncooked jasmine or sticky rice in a small pan over medium heat, shaking often, until it is deep golden and smells nutty. Cool, then grind to a coarse powder in a mortar or spice grinder. It keeps for weeks in a sealed jar and gives the salad its signature toasty crunch.`],
      ['h2', `Cooking and slicing the beef properly`],
      ['p', `Most disappointing beef salads come down to two mistakes: a pan that is not hot enough, and beef sliced with the grain. A lukewarm pan steams the steak grey instead of searing it, so heat it until it is smoking lightly and cook one steak at a time. Resting matters too. Cut a steak straight off the heat and the juices run all over the board instead of staying in the meat.`],
      ['p', `To find the grain, look at the cooked steak and you will see fine lines running in one direction. Turn the steak so those lines run left to right in front of you, then slice straight down across them. With flank or skirt this is essential; with rump it still makes a noticeable difference. Slices about 5 mm thick are ideal for a salad.`],
      ['h2', `Variations to try`],
      ['ul', [
        `Grilled over charcoal: the smoky flavour is the closest you will get to a Bangkok street stall.`,
        `With glass noodles: soak vermicelli in hot water, drain and toss through for a more filling meal.`,
        `Waterfall beef (nam tok): slice the beef more thickly, add extra toasted rice powder and chilli flakes, and skip the tomato.`,
        `With green mango or green papaya: shred and toss through for extra crunch and sourness.`,
        `Lettuce cups: serve the dressed beef in cos or iceberg leaves as a starter.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Serving, storing and leftovers`],
      ['p', `Serve the salad with steamed jasmine rice or sticky rice. If you are cooking ahead, keep the beef, vegetables and dressing separate, and toss them together just before serving so the herbs stay bright. Leftover sliced beef keeps for up to two days in a sealed container in the fridge, and it makes an excellent filling for rice paper rolls or a quick fried rice.`],
      ['h2', `Thai beef salad near you, made at home`],
      ['p', `A restaurant version costs about as much as a whole steak, and making it at home lets you choose the cut and how pink it is. The beef meat price varies a lot between cuts, so compare the price per kilo on each product page: rump and flank give the best value for this dish, while porterhouse is the treat option. You will find every steak cut in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), all certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled Australia-wide.`],
      ['p', `Orders are delivered from our depot in Greenacre, Sydney, with free delivery on orders over $500. If you enjoy this recipe, try our guide to the [best beef for stir fry](/blog/best-beef-for-stir-fry/) or the [satay beef](/blog/satay-beef/) recipe next.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-massaman-curry'),
    title: 'Beef Massaman Curry: A Slow-Cooked Recipe With the Right Cut',
    seoTitle: 'Tender Beef Massaman Curry Recipe',
    excerpt: `Our beef massaman curry recipe: chuck or brisket simmered in coconut milk with potatoes and peanuts until spoon-tender, plus which halal cuts work best.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef massaman curry is the gentlest of the Thai curries: rich with coconut milk, fragrant with cinnamon, cardamom and star anise, sweet-sour from tamarind and palm sugar, and finished with potatoes and roasted peanuts. It has deep roots in Persian and Indian cooking, which is why it tastes warmer and more aromatic than a green or red curry. It is also one of the most forgiving curries you can make, because it is built around a slow simmer that turns a tough, inexpensive cut into something you can cut with a spoon.`],
      ['p', `This guide gives you a dependable beef massaman curry recipe, explains which cuts to buy and why, and covers stovetop, oven and slow cooker methods.`],
      ['h2', `Why massaman beef curry needs a slow-cooking cut`],
      ['p', `A massaman beef curry is braised, not stir-fried. The meat sits in a gently bubbling sauce for two hours or more, and over that time the collagen in hard-working muscles melts into gelatin, which makes the meat soft and gives the sauce body. Lean, quick-cooking steaks such as porterhouse or eye fillet do the opposite: they tighten and dry out in a long simmer, and you will have paid more for a worse result.`],
      ['p', `The best cuts are well marbled and contain plenty of connective tissue. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) is the classic choice. [Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/), cut from the shin, gives the most unctuous sauce. [Beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) are a luxury option that become extraordinarily tender, and point end brisket works well too. If you want to skip the knife work, [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) is already cut into curry-sized pieces.`],
      ['h3', `What about thin sliced beef?`],
      ['p', `Thin sliced beef is sometimes used in quick, restaurant-style massaman made in 20 minutes, but it gives a very different dish: the sauce has little body and the beef can turn chewy. If you are short on time, cook a red or panang curry with sliced beef instead, and save massaman for a slow afternoon. Large chunks of about 4 cm hold together best in a long braise.`],
      ['h2', `Beef massaman curry recipe`],
      ['p', `This beef massaman curry recipe serves four to six. It uses store-bought massaman paste for convenience; check the label, because a few brands include shrimp paste, which is fine for a halal kitchen, but you should confirm the paste has no non-halal additives.`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak, gravy beef or cheeks, cut into 4 cm chunks`,
        `2 x 400 ml cans coconut milk (do not shake them)`,
        `3 to 4 tablespoons massaman curry paste`,
        `1 onion, cut into wedges`,
        `500 g waxy potatoes, peeled and cut into large chunks`,
        `1 cinnamon stick, 3 cardamom pods and 1 star anise`,
        `2 tablespoons tamarind paste`,
        `2 tablespoons fish sauce and 1 to 2 tablespoons palm sugar`,
        `1/2 cup roasted unsalted peanuts`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Open the cans and spoon the thick cream from the top into a heavy pot. Simmer it over medium heat for a few minutes until the oil starts to separate, then add the curry paste and fry, stirring, for 2 to 3 minutes until fragrant. Add the beef and stir to coat. Pour in the remaining coconut milk plus about 1 cup of water, then add the whole spices, tamarind, fish sauce and sugar. Bring to a gentle simmer, cover with the lid slightly ajar and cook on low for 1.5 hours.`],
      ['p', `Add the potatoes and onion and simmer for another 45 minutes to 1 hour, until the beef breaks apart with a spoon and the potatoes are tender. Stir in the peanuts, then taste and balance: more fish sauce for salt, tamarind for sourness, sugar for sweetness. Rest for 10 minutes before serving.`],
      ['h2', `Oven and slow cooker methods`],
      ['p', `In the oven, follow the recipe to the point where everything is simmering, then cover and transfer to a 160°C oven for about 2.5 hours, adding the potatoes for the last hour. Oven braising gives very even heat and almost no risk of catching on the bottom.`],
      ['p', `In a slow cooker, fry the paste in the coconut cream on the stove first (this step builds most of the flavour), then transfer everything except the peanuts to the slow cooker. Cook on low for 7 to 8 hours or high for 4 to 5. Because nothing evaporates in a slow cooker, use only half a cup of water, or none at all.`],
      ['h2', `Getting the balance right`],
      ['p', `Massaman should be sweeter and milder than most Thai curries, but it should never taste flat. If yours does, it almost always needs salt (fish sauce) and acid (tamarind or a squeeze of lime). Taste near the end, not at the start, because the sauce reduces and concentrates as it cooks. If the sauce is too thin, simmer it uncovered for 15 minutes; if too thick, loosen it with a splash of water or stock.`],
      ['h2', `Serving suggestions`],
      ['ul', [
        `Steamed jasmine rice is the classic partner.`,
        `Roti or flatbread is perfect for mopping up the sauce.`,
        `A quick cucumber relish (cucumber, shallot, vinegar and sugar) cuts the richness.`,
        `Scatter with fried shallots and coriander for colour and crunch.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Making ahead and freezing`],
      ['p', `Like most braises, this curry tastes even better the next day once the spices have settled. Cool it quickly, refrigerate it in a covered container and use within three days. It freezes well for up to three months, although the potatoes can turn slightly grainy; if you plan to freeze it, you can leave the potatoes out and add freshly cooked ones when reheating.`],
      ['h2', `Beef massaman curry questions`],
      ['h3', `Can I make massaman paste from scratch?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Dry-roast dried chillies, coriander seeds, cumin, cloves, cardamom and cinnamon, then pound or blend them with roasted shallots, garlic, lemongrass, galangal and a little salt. Homemade paste has a brighter, more aromatic flavour, and you control exactly what goes into it.`],
      ['h3', `Why is my massaman beef still tough?`],
      ['p', `It simply needs more time. Tough beef in a braise means the connective tissue has not finished breaking down. Keep simmering gently and test every 20 minutes; chuck usually needs at least two hours, and cheeks can take three.`],
      ['h2', `Buying beef for curry: price and value`],
      ['p', `Slow-cooking cuts are among the best-value meat you can buy, which makes curry a smart way to feed a crowd. The beef meat price in Australia moves with the season and the cut, so compare the price per kilo on each product page before you order. Chuck and gravy beef are usually the most economical; cheeks cost more but reward you with a remarkable texture. For big gatherings, our [diced beef carton](/product/beef-diced-carton/) is ready to cook straight from the pack.`],
      ['p', `All our beef is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. Browse the full [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), or try the [beef rendang recipe](/blog/beef-rendang-recipe/) next if you enjoy a rich, slow-cooked curry.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-rendang-recipe'),
    title: 'Beef Rendang Recipe: Malaysian Dry Curry Made Step by Step',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Rendang Recipe: Tender Malaysian Curry',
    excerpt: `A beef rendang recipe in the Malaysian style: the spice paste, toasted coconut, the long reduction and the best halal beef cuts for a rich, tender curry.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Rendang is not a curry you rush. It starts like a soupy coconut braise and ends, hours later, as dark, glossy beef coated in a thick, caramelised paste, with almost no liquid left in the pan. That transformation is what makes it one of the most celebrated dishes in Malaysia and Indonesia, and it is surprisingly achievable at home with a little patience. This beef rendang recipe walks you through every stage, explains which cut to buy and shows you how to tell when it is ready.`],
      ['h2', `What is rendang?`],
      ['p', `Rendang comes from the Minangkabau people of West Sumatra, and spread across Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore, where it is cooked for Hari Raya, weddings and family celebrations. Indonesian rendang is often cooked until very dry and dark. A beef rendang Malaysian recipe tends to be a little moister, with more of the coconut sauce clinging to the meat, and it usually includes kerisik, a paste of toasted grated coconut that thickens the sauce and adds a nutty depth.`],
      ['p', `Both versions share the same principle: aromatics and spices are slowly cooked down in coconut milk with the beef until the fat separates and fries the paste. That final frying stage is where the deep colour and flavour come from.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef for curry like rendang`],
      ['p', `The best beef for curry that cooks for three hours or more is a cut with plenty of connective tissue and some marbling. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) is the traditional favourite and holds together well. [Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) from the shin gives a silkier result, and [beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) become meltingly soft. Brisket, especially the [point end](/product/beef-point-end-brisket/), also works beautifully.`],
      ['p', `Avoid lean, fast-cooking steaks. They dry out long before the sauce has reduced. Cut the beef into large pieces of about 4 to 5 cm, because they shrink as they cook and smaller pieces can fall apart into shreds.`],
      ['h2', `Recipe for beef rendang: ingredients`],
      ['p', `This recipe for beef rendang serves six. The spice paste is the heart of the dish, so make it fresh if you can.`],
      ['h3', `Spice paste`],
      ['ul', [
        `10 to 15 dried chillies, soaked in hot water and drained (fewer for a milder curry)`,
        `6 red shallots and 4 cloves garlic`,
        `4 cm piece each of ginger and galangal`,
        `3 stalks lemongrass, white part only`,
        `3 cm piece fresh turmeric, or 1 teaspoon ground turmeric`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Curry`],
      ['ul', [
        `1.2 kg chuck, gravy beef or beef cheeks, cut into large chunks`,
        `2 x 400 ml cans coconut milk`,
        `1 cup water`,
        `2 lemongrass stalks, bruised and tied in a knot`,
        `6 kaffir lime leaves, finely shredded`,
        `1 cinnamon stick, 3 cloves, 3 star anise and 4 cardamom pods`,
        `2 tablespoons tamarind paste`,
        `1 tablespoon palm sugar, and salt to taste`,
        `1/2 cup desiccated or grated coconut, for the kerisik`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Beef rendang Malaysian recipe: the method`],
      ['p', `Blend the spice paste ingredients with a splash of oil until smooth. Heat 3 tablespoons of oil in a large, heavy pot or wok and fry the paste with the whole spices over medium heat for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring often, until it darkens slightly and the oil begins to separate. Do not hurry this step; raw paste tastes harsh.`],
      ['p', `Add the beef and stir to coat. Pour in the coconut milk and water, add the lemongrass, lime leaves, tamarind and sugar, and bring to a simmer. Cook uncovered on a low heat for about 2 hours, stirring every 15 minutes or so. The liquid will slowly reduce.`],
      ['p', `Meanwhile, make the kerisik: dry-fry the coconut in a pan over medium-low heat, stirring constantly, until evenly deep golden brown. Pound it in a mortar or blitz it in a grinder while still warm until it turns into an oily paste. Stir the kerisik into the pot.`],
      ['p', `For the final 30 to 60 minutes, stir more often. As the liquid disappears, the oil separates and the beef begins to fry in its own paste. It is ready when the sauce is thick and dark brown, the oil glistens around the edges and the beef is tender. Season with salt and a little more sugar if needed.`],
      ['h2', `Common rendang mistakes`],
      ['ul', [
        `High heat: the sauce catches and burns long before the beef is tender. Keep the heat low and steady.`,
        `Too little stirring at the end: the reduction stage needs your attention every few minutes.`,
        `Lean beef: it turns dry and stringy. Use chuck, shin, cheeks or brisket.`,
        `Skipping the kerisik: it is what gives Malaysian rendang its body and nutty flavour.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Slow cooker and pressure cooker options`],
      ['p', `You can do the long braise in a slow cooker (about 7 hours on low) after frying the paste on the stove, but you will need to finish the curry in a wide pan on the stove to reduce the sauce, because slow cookers trap moisture. A pressure cooker cuts the braise to about 45 minutes at high pressure; again, finish it in an open pan until the sauce is thick and the oil separates.`],
      ['h2', `Serving and storing`],
      ['p', `Serve rendang with steamed rice, nasi lemak or ketupat (compressed rice cakes), with cucumber and a sambal on the side. Rendang was originally a way to preserve meat, and it keeps well: up to four days in the fridge and three months in the freezer. Many cooks think it tastes best on the second day.`],
      ['h2', `Beef rendang questions`],
      ['h3', `How long does rendang take?`],
      ['p', `Allow three to four hours from start to finish on the stove, most of it hands-off simmering. The final reduction needs the most attention, so plan to be in the kitchen for the last 45 minutes.`],
      ['h3', `Is rendang very spicy?`],
      ['p', `It can be, but you control the heat with the number of dried chillies. Five or six chillies give a gentle warmth suitable for most families; fifteen give a properly fiery rendang. Removing the seeds also lowers the heat without losing the colour.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make rendang without kerisik?`],
      ['p', `You can, and many Indonesian cooks do, but the sauce will be thinner and less nutty. If you skip it, reduce the curry a little further so it still coats the beef.`],
      ['h2', `Where to buy beef for rendang`],
      ['p', `If you have been searching for beef steak near me for a curry night, remember that the cheaper braising cuts are the right buy here, not premium steaks. You will find chuck, gravy beef, cheeks, brisket and [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), all certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. For another slow-simmered favourite, try our [beef massaman curry](/blog/beef-massaman-curry/).`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-curry-recipe'),
    title: 'Beef Curry Recipe: An Easy, Tender Everyday Curry',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Beef Curry Recipe for Tender Beef',
    excerpt: `A simple beef curry recipe for tender results every time: the right cut, the spice base, stovetop and slow cooker timings, and how to fix a flat sauce.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Every family has its own beef curry, and that is exactly how it should be. Some like it mild and creamy, some hot and tomato-rich, some loaded with potatoes. What every good version shares is a well-cooked spice base and beef that has simmered long enough to become tender. This beef curry recipe is a flexible, everyday curry in the North Indian home style, and the techniques in it apply to almost any curry you cook.`],
      ['h2', `What makes a beef curry tender`],
      ['p', `Tenderness in a beef curry comes from time, not from expensive meat. Cuts from the shoulder, shin and chest are full of connective tissue, which is tough when cooked quickly but melts into rich gelatin after one and a half to three hours of gentle simmering. That gelatin is also what gives a curry sauce its glossy body. Making beef curry with a lean steak is a false economy: it costs more and turns dry and chewy in the pot.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef for slow cooker and stovetop curries`],
      ['p', `The best beef for slow cooker curries and stovetop braises are the same hard-working cuts. Our favourites:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/): the all-rounder, well marbled with a beefy flavour.`,
        `[Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/): shin meat with lots of connective tissue, for a silky sauce.`,
        `[Chuck tender](/product/beef-chuck-tender/): a leaner chuck muscle that still braises well.`,
        `[Beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/): the most luxurious result, almost spoonable.`,
        `[Point end brisket](/product/beef-point-end-brisket/): rich and well marbled.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `If you would rather not cut the meat yourself, our [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) is ready to go. For curry, aim for pieces around 3 to 4 cm.`],
      ['h2', `Beef curry recipe`],
      ['p', `This recipe serves four to six.`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak or gravy beef, cut into 3 to 4 cm pieces`,
        `3 tablespoons oil or ghee`,
        `2 large onions, finely chopped`,
        `1 tablespoon each grated garlic and ginger`,
        `2 green chillies, slit (optional)`,
        `2 teaspoons ground coriander, 1 teaspoon ground cumin, 1 teaspoon chilli powder and 1/2 teaspoon turmeric`,
        `1 cinnamon stick, 4 cardamom pods, 4 cloves and 2 bay leaves`,
        `400 g can crushed tomatoes`,
        `1 cup plain yoghurt (optional, for a richer sauce)`,
        `1 to 2 teaspoons garam masala, salt, and fresh coriander to finish`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Heat the oil or ghee in a heavy pot over medium heat. Add the whole spices and let them sizzle for 30 seconds. Add the onions and a pinch of salt and cook, stirring often, for 15 to 20 minutes until deep golden brown. This is the most important step in making beef curry: well-browned onions give the sauce its sweetness and colour.`],
      ['p', `Add the garlic, ginger and chillies and cook for 2 minutes. Add the ground spices with a splash of water and cook for another minute or two until the oil starts to separate. Add the tomatoes and cook for 8 to 10 minutes until thick and jammy.`],
      ['p', `Add the beef, turn up the heat and stir for 5 minutes until the meat is coated and lightly sealed. If using yoghurt, stir it in a spoonful at a time. Add about 1 cup of water, bring to a simmer, cover and cook on low for 1.5 to 2 hours, stirring occasionally, until the beef is tender. Stir in the garam masala, check the salt and scatter with coriander.`],
      ['h2', `Slow cooker method`],
      ['p', `Brown the onions and cook the spice base on the stove as above: a slow cooker cannot brown, and skipping this step gives a pale, flat curry. Transfer the base and the beef to the slow cooker with only half a cup of water, as slow cookers lose very little liquid. Cook on low for 7 to 8 hours or high for 4 to 5. If the sauce is thin at the end, take the lid off for the final 30 minutes or reduce it in a pan.`],
      ['h2', `Fixing a beef curry that tastes flat`],
      ['ul', [
        `Under-salted: salt is the most common culprit. Add it gradually and taste.`,
        `No brightness: a squeeze of lemon juice or a spoon of tamarind lifts the sauce.`,
        `Raw spice taste: the ground spices were not cooked long enough. Simmer a little longer.`,
        `Too hot: stir in yoghurt, cream or coconut milk.`,
        `Watery: simmer uncovered for 15 to 20 minutes.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Variations`],
      ['p', `Add cubed potatoes for the last 40 minutes for a hearty aloo gosht-style curry. Stir in spinach at the end for a saag-style finish, or add chickpeas to stretch the meat further. For a South Indian flavour, replace the tomatoes with coconut milk and add curry leaves and mustard seeds. The same base also works with lamb or goat; see our [diced lamb recipes](/blog/diced-lamb-recipes/) and [goat curry cuts guide](/blog/goat-meat-curry-cuts-guide/).`],
      ['h2', `Storing and freezing`],
      ['p', `Beef curry keeps for three days in the fridge and three months in the freezer. Cool it quickly by spreading it into a shallow container. Reheat until piping hot all the way through, adding a splash of water if the sauce has thickened.`],
      ['h2', `Beef curry questions`],
      ['h3', `How long should beef curry cook?`],
      ['p', `On the stove, chuck and gravy beef need about 1.5 to 2 hours at a gentle simmer; cheeks can take up to 3 hours. Test a piece: it should break apart easily with a spoon. In a slow cooker, allow 7 to 8 hours on low.`],
      ['h3', `Should I brown the beef first?`],
      ['p', `Browning adds flavour, but in a well-spiced curry it is less critical than browning the onions. If you have time, sear the beef in batches before adding it to the spice base. If you are in a hurry, adding it raw to the masala and stirring over a high heat for five minutes works well.`],
      ['h3', `Can I use frozen beef?`],
      ['p', `Yes, but thaw it completely in the fridge first so it cooks evenly and you can pat it dry. Adding frozen meat to a curry drops the temperature sharply and leaves the sauce watery.`],
      ['h3', `How much beef curry per person?`],
      ['p', `Allow about 200 g of raw boneless beef per person for a main meal with rice and bread, or 150 g if you are serving several dishes.`],
      ['h2', `Buying beef for curry`],
      ['p', `Searching for beef brisket near me before a curry night? Brisket is a great curry cut, and we deliver it, along with chuck, shin and cheeks, anywhere in Australia from our depot in Greenacre, Sydney. Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia. Browse the full [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/), or for catering quantities see our [bulk meat boxes](/shop/bulk-meat-boxes/).`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-korma'),
    title: 'Beef Korma: A Mild, Creamy Curry Recipe for the Whole Family',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Beef Korma Recipe: Mild and Creamy',
    excerpt: `A mild, creamy beef korma with yoghurt, cashews and gentle spices, plus slow cooker timings and the best halal cuts for melt-in-the-mouth beef.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef korma is the curry for people who think they do not like curry. It is mild, fragrant and creamy, built on yoghurt, ground nuts and gently cooked onions rather than chilli heat. That makes it a favourite with children and anyone who prefers aromatic spice to fire. A proper korma is not bland, though: cardamom, cinnamon, cloves and a little saffron or rose water give it a perfumed richness that a jar sauce rarely matches.`],
      ['p', `This guide gives you a reliable beef korma recipe, a slow cooker version, and advice on choosing the cut.`],
      ['h2', `What is korma?`],
      ['p', `Korma comes from the Mughal courts of northern India, where it described a style of cooking: meat braised slowly in yoghurt, cream or stock with nuts and whole spices. Traditional kormas were often made with lamb or mutton, but beef korma is now hugely popular in Pakistan, Bangladesh and in curry houses worldwide. The style varies from rich and pale to golden and slightly spiced, but it is always gentle rather than hot.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef for slow cooking a korma`],
      ['p', `The best beef for slow cooking in a creamy sauce is a cut that stays moist and turns tender after two hours. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) is ideal. [Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) is even richer, and [beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) become wonderfully soft. Because korma has a delicate sauce, trim away any large pieces of hard fat or sinew before cooking. If you want to save time, our [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) is cut to curry size.`],
      ['h2', `Beef korma recipe`],
      ['p', `This beef korma recipe serves four to six.`],
      ['h3', `Marinade`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak or gravy beef, cut into 3 to 4 cm pieces`,
        `1 cup thick plain yoghurt`,
        `1 tablespoon each grated garlic and ginger`,
        `1 teaspoon salt and 1/2 teaspoon turmeric`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Sauce`],
      ['ul', [
        `3 tablespoons ghee or oil`,
        `2 large onions, very finely sliced`,
        `1/2 cup cashews or blanched almonds, soaked in hot water for 20 minutes`,
        `6 green cardamom pods, 1 cinnamon stick, 4 cloves and 2 bay leaves`,
        `2 teaspoons ground coriander and 1 teaspoon ground cumin`,
        `1/2 teaspoon mild chilli powder (optional)`,
        `1/2 cup cream or coconut cream`,
        `A pinch of saffron soaked in 2 tablespoons warm milk, and 1/2 teaspoon garam masala`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Mix the beef with the marinade ingredients and refrigerate for at least an hour, or overnight. Blend the soaked nuts with a little water to a smooth paste.`],
      ['p', `Heat the ghee in a heavy pot and fry the whole spices for 30 seconds. Add the onions and cook over medium-low heat for 15 to 20 minutes until soft and pale golden; for a classic korma, do not let them darken too much. Add the ground spices and cook for 1 minute.`],
      ['p', `Add the beef with all its marinade. Stir over medium heat for 5 minutes, then reduce the heat, add half a cup of water, cover and simmer very gently for 1.5 to 2 hours until tender. Keep the heat low so the yoghurt does not split. Stir in the nut paste, cream and saffron milk, simmer for 10 minutes more, then finish with garam masala and taste for salt.`],
      ['h2', `Beef korma slow cooker method`],
      ['p', `For a beef korma slow cooker version, marinate the beef as above and cook the onions and spices on the stove first. Transfer everything except the nut paste, cream and saffron to the slow cooker. Cook on low for 7 to 8 hours. Stir in the nut paste, cream and saffron for the final 30 minutes with the lid off. Slow cookers are gentle, so the yoghurt rarely splits, but the sauce can be thin; the nut paste thickens it nicely.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for a silky korma`],
      ['ul', [
        `Use full-fat yoghurt; low-fat versions split easily.`,
        `Bring the yoghurt to room temperature before cooking.`,
        `Cook on the lowest heat that keeps a gentle bubble.`,
        `Soak the nuts well so the paste is completely smooth.`,
        `Add a teaspoon of sugar if the yoghurt tastes very sour.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `What to serve with beef korma`],
      ['p', `Korma pairs beautifully with saffron or jeera rice, naan or paratha. Offer a sharper side to balance the richness: a kachumber salad of cucumber, tomato and onion with lemon, or a simple carrot pickle. Leftovers keep for three days in the fridge and freeze for up to three months, though cream-based sauces can look slightly grainy after freezing; a vigorous stir while reheating brings them back.`],
      ['h2', `Beef korma questions`],
      ['h3', `Is beef korma spicy?`],
      ['p', `No. Korma is one of the mildest curries. It is fragrant and gently spiced, with almost no chilli heat unless you add some. That makes it a popular first curry for children and anyone sensitive to heat.`],
      ['h3', `Why did my korma sauce split?`],
      ['p', `Yoghurt splits when it gets too hot too quickly or when low-fat yoghurt is used. Use full-fat yoghurt at room temperature, add it gradually over low heat, and stir constantly. If it does split, a splash of cream and a quick whisk often brings it back together.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make korma without nuts?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Replace the cashew or almond paste with extra cream or coconut cream, or blend in a few tablespoons of desiccated coconut or toasted sunflower seeds for body. The texture will be slightly lighter but still creamy.`],
      ['h3', `What is the difference between korma and butter chicken?`],
      ['p', `Butter chicken is built on a tomato and butter sauce and is usually tangier and redder. Korma is built on yoghurt, onions and nuts, so it is paler, nuttier and more perfumed. Both are mild.`],
      ['h3', `How far ahead can I make it?`],
      ['p', `Up to two days. Like most braises, korma improves overnight. Reheat it gently and add a splash of water or cream to loosen the sauce.`],
      ['h3', `Which rice goes best with korma?`],
      ['p', `Fragrant basmati is the natural partner. For a special occasion, cook it with a few cardamom pods, a cinnamon stick and a pinch of saffron so the rice echoes the spices in the sauce without overpowering it.`],
      ['h2', `Not a curry night?`],
      ['p', `If tonight calls for something quicker and you have been searching beef burger near me, our [beef patties](/product/beef-patties-chuck-and-brisket/) made from chuck and brisket cook in under 10 minutes. And for curry nights, you will find chuck, gravy beef, cheeks and diced beef in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Everything is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. For a spicier dish, try our [beef vindaloo](/blog/beef-vindaloo/) next.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-vindaloo'),
    title: 'Beef Vindaloo: A Hot and Tangy Goan-Style Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Easy Beef Vindaloo Recipe: Hot and Tangy',
    excerpt: `A beef vindaloo with a vinegar and chilli marinade, slow-simmered until tender: the spice paste, the best halal cuts and how to control the heat.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Vindaloo has a reputation as the hottest curry on the menu, but in its home state of Goa it is just as much about tang as heat. The defining flavour is vinegar, balanced with garlic, dried chillies and warm spices, and the meat is marinated in that mixture before a long, slow simmer. The result is a sharp, deeply savoury curry that is far more interesting than simple fire. This beef vindaloo recipe keeps the traditional balance, and lets you set the heat where you like it.`],
      ['h2', `Where vindaloo comes from`],
      ['p', `Vindaloo grew out of Goa's Portuguese history: a dish of meat preserved in vinegar and garlic met local chillies, tamarind and spices, and became something new. British curry houses later turned it into a byword for extreme heat, often with potatoes added because "aloo" sounded like part of the name. The Goan original has no potato requirement, though adding some is perfectly fine.`],
      ['h2', `Beef cuts best for vindaloo`],
      ['p', `Which beef cuts best suit vindaloo? The same ones that suit any long braise. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) gives a classic result. [Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) and [beef shin](/product/beef-shin-hammer/) produce a rich, gelatinous sauce. Point end brisket and [beef cheeks](/product/buy-beef-cheeks/) also work well. A little fat is welcome here, because it carries the chilli and spice flavours. Cut the beef into 4 cm pieces; our [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) is a convenient shortcut.`],
      ['h2', `Beef vindaloo recipe`],
      ['p', `This beef vindaloo recipe serves four to six. Start the marinade the night before if you can.`],
      ['h3', `Vindaloo paste`],
      ['ul', [
        `8 to 12 dried Kashmiri chillies (milder, deep red) plus 2 to 6 hotter dried chillies, to taste`,
        `1 teaspoon cumin seeds, 1 teaspoon black peppercorns and 6 cloves`,
        `1 cinnamon stick, broken`,
        `1 teaspoon turmeric`,
        `8 cloves garlic and a 5 cm piece of ginger`,
        `1/3 cup malt vinegar or white vinegar`,
        `1 tablespoon tamarind paste and 1 tablespoon brown sugar`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Curry`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak or gravy beef, cut into 4 cm pieces`,
        `3 tablespoons oil`,
        `2 large onions, finely sliced`,
        `1 teaspoon mustard seeds`,
        `2 tomatoes, chopped`,
        `Salt, and 400 g potatoes (optional)`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Toast the dried chillies and whole spices in a dry pan for a minute until fragrant, then soak the chillies in the vinegar for 20 minutes. Blend everything for the paste until smooth. Rub half the paste into the beef with a teaspoon of salt and marinate in the fridge for at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.`],
      ['p', `Heat the oil in a heavy pot, add the mustard seeds and, when they pop, the onions. Cook for 15 minutes until deep golden. Add the remaining paste and fry for 5 minutes until it darkens and the oil separates. Add the tomatoes and cook down for 5 minutes, then add the beef and its marinade. Stir well, add 1 cup of water, cover and simmer on low for 1.5 to 2 hours, until the beef is tender. Add the potatoes, if using, for the final 40 minutes. Taste and adjust the salt, sugar and vinegar: the curry should be noticeably tangy.`],
      ['h2', `Recipe beef vindaloo variations: controlling the heat`],
      ['p', `If you are adapting any recipe beef vindaloo style for a mixed table, the chillies are your dial. Kashmiri chillies give colour with modest heat, so increase them and reduce the hot chillies for a milder curry that still looks the part. Removing the seeds before soaking also tames the heat. If the finished curry is too hot, stir in a spoonful of yoghurt per serve, or serve it with plenty of rice and a cooling raita.`],
      ['h2', `Slow cooker method`],
      ['p', `Marinate the beef and cook the onion and paste base on the stove as above. Transfer to the slow cooker with only half a cup of water and cook on low for 8 hours. Vinegar flavours stay sharp in a slow cooker, so taste before adding more at the end.`],
      ['h2', `Why vindaloo tastes better tomorrow`],
      ['p', `The vinegar and spices continue to soak into the meat as it cools, and the sauce mellows and deepens overnight. Vindaloo keeps well in the fridge for up to four days and freezes for three months. Cool it quickly, store it in a sealed container, and reheat until piping hot.`],
      ['h2', `What to serve with beef vindaloo`],
      ['ul', [
        `Plain basmati rice or Goan red rice`,
        `Soft bread rolls (pão) to soak up the sauce, as they do in Goa`,
        `Cucumber raita or plain yoghurt`,
        `A crunchy onion and tomato salad with lemon`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Leftovers make a brilliant topping for the beef burger: pile shredded vindaloo beef on a grilled [beef patty](/product/beef-patties-chuck-and-brisket/) with a spoon of raita and some crisp onion.`],
      ['h2', `Beef vindaloo questions`],
      ['h3', `Is vindaloo the hottest curry?`],
      ['p', `In many restaurants it is near the top of the heat scale, but an authentic Goan vindaloo is about balance: tangy, garlicky and spicy rather than painfully hot. At home, the heat is entirely up to you, because it comes from the chillies you choose.`],
      ['h3', `Which vinegar should I use?`],
      ['p', `Malt vinegar or plain white vinegar both work well. In Goa, cooks often use coconut toddy vinegar or cane vinegar if they can find it. Avoid balsamic, which is too sweet and dark for this dish.`],
      ['h3', `How long should I marinate the beef?`],
      ['p', `At least 4 hours, and overnight is better. The vinegar and spices begin to season the meat and make the final curry noticeably deeper. Do not go beyond 24 hours, as the acid can make the surface of the meat mushy.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make vindaloo in a pressure cooker?`],
      ['p', `Yes. After frying the paste and adding the beef, cook at high pressure for about 35 to 40 minutes, then release naturally. Simmer uncovered afterwards if the sauce needs thickening.`],
      ['h3', `Does vindaloo always have potatoes?`],
      ['p', `No. The name comes from Portuguese words for vinegar and garlic, not from "aloo". Potatoes are a popular addition in curry houses, but the traditional dish does not require them.`],
      ['h3', `Can I freeze the vindaloo paste?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Make a double batch of paste and freeze half in an ice cube tray. Once frozen, store the cubes in a sealed bag for up to three months and use them straight from the freezer next time.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for curry`],
      ['p', `Chuck, gravy beef, shin, cheeks and diced beef are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide, with free delivery on orders over $500. For more curry ideas, read our [beef madras](/blog/beef-madras/) and [beef korma](/blog/beef-korma/) recipes.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-madras'),
    title: 'Beef Madras: A Rich, Hot Curry House Classic at Home',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Madras Curry Recipe: Easy and Rich',
    excerpt: `A curry-house beef madras at home: a hot, tangy sauce with chilli, tamarind and curry leaves, the best halal cuts to slow cook, and stovetop timings.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef madras is the curry-house favourite for people who like it hot but still want flavour. It is a deep red, fairly thick curry with plenty of chilli, a sour edge from tamarind or lemon, and the earthy warmth of cumin, coriander and fenugreek. The name nods to the city now called Chennai, though the dish as most of us know it was shaped in British and Australian curry houses. Made at home with slow-cooked beef, it is far better than any takeaway.`],
      ['h2', `What makes a madras different`],
      ['p', `Compared with a korma, a beef madras curry is hotter and sharper, with no cream or nuts. Compared with a vindaloo, it uses less vinegar and relies more on tamarind, curry leaves and mustard seeds, which gives it a South Indian accent. Many recipes add a little coconut milk at the end to round off the heat. It sits in the middle of the heat scale on most menus: hot, but not punishing.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef cut to slow cook for madras`],
      ['p', `The best beef cut to slow cook for any curry has connective tissue that melts during cooking. Choose [chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) for a well-marbled, classic result, [gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) for a silky sauce, or [beef shin](/product/beef-shin-hammer/) for maximum richness. Beef cheeks are a superb, indulgent option. Our [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/) is ready-cut for convenience. Avoid stir-fry strips and premium steaks: they toughen in a long simmer.`],
      ['h2', `Madras beef recipe`],
      ['p', `This madras beef recipe serves four to six.`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak or gravy beef, cut into 3 to 4 cm pieces`,
        `3 tablespoons oil`,
        `1 teaspoon mustard seeds and 15 to 20 curry leaves`,
        `2 onions, finely chopped`,
        `1 tablespoon each grated garlic and ginger`,
        `2 tablespoons madras curry powder (or the blend below)`,
        `1 to 3 teaspoons hot chilli powder, to taste`,
        `400 g can crushed tomatoes`,
        `2 tablespoons tamarind paste`,
        `1/2 cup coconut milk (optional)`,
        `Salt and 1 teaspoon garam masala`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Homemade madras curry powder`],
      ['p', `Dry-roast 2 tablespoons coriander seeds, 1 tablespoon cumin seeds, 1 teaspoon fenugreek seeds, 1 teaspoon black peppercorns, 1 teaspoon mustard seeds and 4 dried chillies until fragrant. Grind finely and mix with 1 teaspoon turmeric. Freshly ground spices make a striking difference to this curry.`],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Heat the oil in a heavy pot. Add the mustard seeds and, when they crackle, the curry leaves. Add the onions and cook for 12 to 15 minutes until golden. Add the garlic and ginger for 2 minutes, then the curry powder and chilli with a splash of water, stirring for 2 minutes until the oil separates.`],
      ['p', `Add the tomatoes and cook for 8 minutes until thick. Add the beef and stir over a high heat for 5 minutes. Add the tamarind and 1 cup of water, bring to a simmer, cover and cook on low for 1.5 to 2 hours until tender. Stir in the coconut milk, if using, and the garam masala, and simmer uncovered for 10 minutes to thicken. Season with salt.`],
      ['h2', `Getting the heat right`],
      ['p', `A good madras should make you reach for water without stopping you from tasting the spices. Start with 1 teaspoon of chilli powder, taste after an hour, and add more if you want. Remember the heat concentrates as the sauce reduces. Kashmiri chilli powder adds colour without much heat if you want the deep red look with less burn.`],
      ['h2', `Slow cooker and oven methods`],
      ['p', `Cook the base on the stove, then transfer to a slow cooker with only half a cup of water for 7 to 8 hours on low. In the oven, braise covered at 160°C for about 2 hours. Either way, the final uncovered simmer on the stove helps concentrate the sauce.`],
      ['h2', `Serving suggestions`],
      ['ul', [
        `Basmati or coconut rice`,
        `Paratha, roti or naan`,
        `Cooling cucumber raita`,
        `Lime pickle or mango chutney`,
        `A simple dal for a vegetarian side`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Takeaway cravings, home-cooked`],
      ['p', `Searches like beef rendang near me and "madras near me" show how often a curry craving strikes. Making it at home with good beef gives you a bigger, better pot for the price of one takeaway meal, and leftovers freeze for up to three months. If rendang is what you are after, try our [beef rendang recipe](/blog/beef-rendang-recipe/).`],
      ['h2', `Beef madras questions`],
      ['h3', `How hot is a madras curry?`],
      ['p', `On most curry-house menus, madras is hot: hotter than a jalfrezi or rogan josh, milder than a vindaloo or phaal. At home you can make it as gentle or as fiery as you like by adjusting the chilli powder.`],
      ['h3', `What gives madras its sour flavour?`],
      ['p', `Tamarind is the traditional souring agent, and many recipes also use a squeeze of lemon. The sourness balances the heat and stops the curry from tasting heavy.`],
      ['h3', `Can I use curry powder from the supermarket?`],
      ['p', `Yes. A good madras curry powder works well. Toast it briefly in the oil to wake up the flavour. Grinding your own, as described above, gives a fresher and more aromatic result if you have the time.`],
      ['h3', `Are curry leaves essential?`],
      ['p', `They are not essential, but they add a distinctive aroma that is hard to replace. Fresh curry leaves keep for weeks in the freezer, so buy a bunch from an Indian grocer and freeze what you do not use.`],
      ['h3', `What drinks suit a madras?`],
      ['p', `A cooling mango lassi, a salted yoghurt drink or simply cold water. Dairy is better than water at calming chilli heat, which is why raita and lassi are traditional partners for hot curries.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make madras with lamb or goat?`],
      ['p', `Absolutely. Diced lamb shoulder or bone-in goat both make excellent madras. Goat may need a little longer to become tender, so test it from the 1 hour 45 minute mark.`],
      ['h3', `How do I stop the spices burning?`],
      ['p', `Add a splash of water with the ground spices and keep stirring over medium heat. The water lets them cook through gently; when it evaporates and the oil separates from the paste, the spices are ready for the tomatoes. If they ever smell acrid, start again, because burnt spices make the whole curry bitter.`],
      ['h2', `Halal beef for curry, delivered`],
      ['p', `You will find chuck, gravy beef, shin, cheeks and diced beef in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia, with free delivery on orders over $500. Cooking for a crowd? Our [diced beef carton](/product/beef-diced-carton/) is made for catering.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-biryani'),
    title: 'Beef Biryani: A Layered Rice Recipe With Tender Spiced Beef',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Biryani Recipe: Tender, Fragrant Rice',
    excerpt: `A step-by-step beef biryani: spiced, tender beef layered with fragrant basmati and slow-steamed (dum), plus the best halal cuts and timings.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `A pot of biryani is a celebration. Layers of fragrant basmati, tender spiced beef, golden fried onions, herbs and saffron are sealed and steamed together until every grain of rice carries the flavour of the meat. It is the centrepiece of Eid tables, weddings and family gatherings from Hyderabad to Karachi to Dhaka, and beef biryani is especially loved in Pakistan, Bangladesh and Kerala. It takes some organisation, but none of the steps is difficult.`],
      ['h2', `How biryani works`],
      ['p', `Most home biryanis follow the "pakki" method: the meat is cooked first into a thick, spiced curry, the rice is par-boiled separately, and then the two are layered and steamed together (the "dum" stage). The steam finishes cooking the rice and lets the aromas of the meat, saffron and fried onions move through the whole pot. This is the most reliable method for beef, because beef needs more cooking time than the rice.`],
      ['h2', `Best part of beef for biryani`],
      ['p', `The best part of beef for biryani is a cut that turns tender in about 1.5 to 2 hours without falling into shreds. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) is the top choice, with bone-in pieces such as [beef osso buco](/product/beef-osso-buco/) adding extra flavour if you like meat on the bone. [Gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) is also excellent. Cut the meat into 4 to 5 cm pieces, or use our ready-cut [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/).`],
      ['h2', `Beef biryani recipe`],
      ['p', `This beef biryani recipe serves six to eight.`],
      ['h3', `Beef masala`],
      ['ul', [
        `1.2 kg chuck or gravy beef, cut into large pieces`,
        `1 cup plain yoghurt`,
        `1 tablespoon each grated garlic and ginger`,
        `2 teaspoons chilli powder, 2 teaspoons ground coriander, 1 teaspoon ground cumin, 1/2 teaspoon turmeric`,
        `2 teaspoons biryani masala or garam masala`,
        `1/4 cup oil or ghee, 3 tomatoes (chopped), 3 green chillies and salt`,
        `Whole spices: 1 cinnamon stick, 6 cardamom pods, 6 cloves, 2 bay leaves`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Rice and layering`],
      ['ul', [
        `4 cups aged basmati rice, washed and soaked for 30 minutes`,
        `4 large onions, thinly sliced and fried until deep golden`,
        `1 cup each chopped mint and coriander`,
        `A large pinch of saffron soaked in 1/4 cup warm milk`,
        `3 tablespoons ghee, and the juice of 1 lemon`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Marinate the beef with the yoghurt, garlic, ginger, ground spices and salt for at least 2 hours. Heat the oil or ghee in a heavy pot, fry the whole spices, then add the beef with its marinade, the tomatoes and chillies. Cook over medium heat for 10 minutes, then cover and simmer on low for 1.5 hours, until the beef is tender and the sauce is thick. Stir in half the fried onions.`],
      ['p', `Bring a large pot of well-salted water to the boil with a few whole spices. Add the drained rice and boil for 5 to 6 minutes, until about 70% cooked: the grains should still have a firm core. Drain.`],
      ['p', `Spread the beef in the bottom of the pot. Scatter with mint, coriander and some fried onions. Layer the rice on top, drizzle with the saffron milk, lemon juice and ghee, and finish with the remaining onions and herbs. Cover with foil and a tight lid. Cook over a very low heat for 25 to 30 minutes (a heat diffuser or flat tawa under the pot helps), or bake at 170°C for 30 minutes. Rest for 10 minutes before opening.`],
      ['h2', `Recipe beef biryani tips`],
      ['ul', [
        `Use aged basmati: it stays separate and elongates beautifully.`,
        `Salt the rice water well; it should taste like the sea.`,
        `Do not fully cook the rice before layering, or it turns mushy during dum.`,
        `Make sure the beef masala is thick, not watery, before layering.`,
        `Mix gently from the side when serving so each plate gets rice and meat.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `Serving biryani`],
      ['p', `Serve with a cooling raita, a simple onion and cucumber salad, and perhaps a boiled egg on top. Biryani keeps in the fridge for three days. Reheat it gently, covered, with a sprinkle of water so the rice steams rather than dries out.`],
      ['h2', `Cooking for a crowd`],
      ['p', `Biryani scales up well, which makes it a favourite for celebrations. If the occasion calls for something else too, you may have been searching beef rendang malay near me for a second dish; our [beef rendang recipe](/blog/beef-rendang-recipe/) can be made a day ahead and pairs well with a biryani spread. For larger events, our [bulk meat boxes](/shop/bulk-meat-boxes/) and [catering packs](/product/goat-curry-cut-catering-pack/) are priced for quantity.`],
      ['h2', `Beef biryani questions`],
      ['h3', `Why is my biryani rice mushy?`],
      ['p', `The rice was cooked too long before layering, or the meat masala was too wet. Par-boil the rice only until it is about 70% cooked, and make sure the beef sauce is thick before you layer. The rice will finish cooking in the steam.`],
      ['h3', `Can I make biryani with uncooked beef?`],
      ['p', `That is the "kacchi" method, where raw marinated meat is layered with rice and slow-cooked together. It works beautifully with tender young goat or lamb, but beef usually needs more cooking time than the rice can tolerate, so the pre-cooked method above is more reliable.`],
      ['h3', `What is biryani masala?`],
      ['p', `It is a spice blend similar to garam masala, often with extra mace, nutmeg, star anise and black cardamom. You can buy it ready-made from Indian grocers or use garam masala with a pinch of nutmeg and mace.`],
      ['h3', `Should I add potatoes?`],
      ['p', `Potatoes are standard in Kolkata-style biryani and popular in many Pakistani homes. Fry large chunks until golden and add them with the meat layer so they absorb the masala.`],
      ['h3', `How much rice per person?`],
      ['p', `About half a cup of uncooked basmati per person is a generous main serve. For a buffet with several other dishes, a third of a cup each is enough.`],
      ['h3', `Can biryani be frozen?`],
      ['p', `Yes, for up to two months. Freeze in portions and reheat covered in the oven or microwave with a splash of water.`],
      ['h3', `How do I seal the pot for dum?`],
      ['p', `Cover the pot tightly with foil and press the lid down on top. Traditional cooks seal the lid with a rope of dough, which works brilliantly and looks dramatic when you break it open at the table.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for biryani`],
      ['p', `Chuck, gravy beef, osso buco and diced beef are all available in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, Australia-wide. Prefer lamb or goat biryani? See our guides to [diced lamb recipes](/blog/diced-lamb-recipes/) and [goat meat curry cuts](/blog/goat-meat-curry-cuts-guide/).`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('rogan-josh-with-beef'),
    title: 'Rogan Josh With Beef: A Kashmiri-Style Curry Recipe',
    seoTitle: 'Rogan Josh With Beef: Easy, Tender Recipe',
    excerpt: `How to make rogan josh with beef: Kashmiri chilli, fennel, ginger and yoghurt, slow-simmered until tender, plus the best halal cuts and slow cooker timings.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Rogan josh is one of the great curries of Kashmir: a deep red, aromatic dish built on Kashmiri chilli, fennel, dried ginger and warm spices, enriched with yoghurt. Traditionally it is made with lamb or mutton, but rogan josh with beef is a delicious and economical alternative, and the slow-cooking cuts of beef take on the spices beautifully. This guide covers a home-style beef rogan josh recipe, the right cut and a slow cooker version.`],
      ['h2', `What is rogan josh?`],
      ['p', `The name is usually explained as "red oil" or "red heat", a reference to the glossy, chilli-tinted fat that rises to the top of the sauce. The deep colour comes mainly from Kashmiri chillies, which are mild but intensely red, and in some versions from a dried flower called ratan jot. The distinctive flavour comes from ground fennel and dried ginger, a combination that sets rogan josh apart from most other curries.`],
      ['p', `Kashmiri Pandit kitchens traditionally make it without onion or garlic, while Muslim Kashmiri versions often include onions. Restaurant versions frequently add tomatoes. All are valid; the recipe below takes a middle path.`],
      ['h2', `Best beef to slow cook for rogan josh`],
      ['p', `The best beef to slow cook here is a well-marbled cut with connective tissue. [Chuck steak](/product/beef-chuck-meat-steak/) is our first choice, and [gravy beef](/product/gravy-beef/) gives a richer, more gelatinous sauce. For a bone-in version that mirrors the traditional mutton dish, use [beef osso buco](/product/beef-osso-buco/): the marrow adds depth. Cut boneless meat into 4 cm pieces, or use our [diced beef](/product/buy-diced-beef-online/).`],
      ['h2', `Beef rogan josh recipe`],
      ['p', `This beef rogan josh recipe serves four to six.`],
      ['ul', [
        `1 kg chuck steak or gravy beef, in 4 cm pieces`,
        `1 cup thick plain yoghurt, whisked`,
        `1/4 cup ghee or oil`,
        `Whole spices: 1 cinnamon stick, 6 green cardamom, 2 black cardamom, 6 cloves, 2 bay leaves`,
        `2 onions, finely sliced (optional)`,
        `1 tablespoon each grated garlic and ginger (optional)`,
        `2 to 3 tablespoons Kashmiri chilli powder`,
        `2 teaspoons ground fennel and 1 teaspoon dried ginger powder`,
        `1 teaspoon ground coriander and 1/2 teaspoon garam masala`,
        `1 cup water or beef stock, and salt`,
      ]],
      ['h3', `Method`],
      ['p', `Heat the ghee in a heavy pot and add the whole spices. When they sizzle, add the beef in batches and brown it well on all sides; remove each batch to a plate. If using onions, cook them in the same pot for 12 to 15 minutes until golden, then add the garlic and ginger for 2 minutes.`],
      ['p', `Lower the heat and stir the Kashmiri chilli powder into a few tablespoons of water to make a paste; add it to the pot and cook for 1 minute (the water stops the chilli burning). Return the beef. Add the yoghurt a spoonful at a time, stirring constantly, so it does not split. Add the fennel, dried ginger and coriander, then the water or stock and salt. Cover and simmer gently for 1.5 to 2 hours until the beef is tender and the red oil rises to the surface. Finish with garam masala.`],
      ['h2', `Beef rogan josh slow cooker method`],
      ['p', `For a beef rogan josh slow cooker version, brown the beef and cook the spices on the stove, then add the yoghurt off the heat and transfer everything to the slow cooker with only half a cup of liquid. Cook on low for 7 to 8 hours. Remove the lid for the final 30 minutes if the sauce needs thickening. Browning first is essential; it gives the curry its depth.`],
      ['h2', `Tips for the best colour and flavour`],
      ['ul', [
        `Use real Kashmiri chilli powder for colour without excessive heat.`,
        `Brown the beef well; pale meat gives a pale sauce.`,
        `Add the yoghurt slowly over low heat to prevent curdling.`,
        `Grind fennel seeds fresh for the most aromatic result.`,
        `Let the curry rest for 20 minutes before serving.`,
      ]],
      ['h2', `What to serve with rogan josh`],
      ['p', `Serve with plain basmati rice, naan or sheermal, and a simple salad. In Kashmir it is part of the wazwan feast alongside other slow-cooked dishes. Leftovers keep for three days in the fridge and freeze for up to three months.`],
      ['h2', `Try a lamb version too`],
      ['p', `For the traditional dish, use [diced lamb](/product/diced-lamb-boneless/) or [lamb shanks](/product/lamb-shanks/) in place of beef and reduce the cooking time a little. And if you landed here searching thai beef salad near me, you will find our quick, fresh [Thai beef salad](/blog/thai-beef-salad/) recipe on the blog too.`],
      ['h2', `Rogan josh questions`],
      ['h3', `Is rogan josh hot?`],
      ['p', `It is usually medium. Kashmiri chillies give it a deep red colour with relatively mild heat, so a rogan josh looks fiery but is milder than a madras or vindaloo. You can add a hotter chilli powder if you prefer.`],
      ['h3', `Can I use paprika instead of Kashmiri chilli?`],
      ['p', `A mix of sweet paprika and a little cayenne is the most common substitute: about two parts paprika to one part cayenne for a similar colour and heat. The flavour will be slightly different but still good.`],
      ['h3', `Do I need tomatoes in rogan josh?`],
      ['p', `Traditional Kashmiri rogan josh does not use tomatoes. Many restaurant versions do, for extra body and tang. If you like that style, add a chopped tomato after the onions and cook it down before adding the yoghurt.`],
      ['h3', `Why add the chilli as a paste?`],
      ['p', `Ground chilli burns very quickly in hot fat, which makes the sauce bitter. Mixing it with a little water first protects it while it cooks and helps the curry keep its bright red colour.`],
      ['h3', `What is the best rice for rogan josh?`],
      ['p', `Long-grain basmati is ideal. Rinse it until the water runs clear and cook it by the absorption method for fluffy, separate grains.`],
      ['h3', `Can I cook it ahead?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Rogan josh improves after a night in the fridge. Cool it quickly, keep it covered and use it within three days.`],
      ['h3', `What is black cardamom?`],
      ['p', `Black cardamom is a larger, darker pod with a smoky, camphor-like aroma. One or two pods add depth to rogan josh. If you cannot find it, leave it out and add one extra green cardamom pod instead; the curry will still taste excellent.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef for rogan josh`],
      ['p', `Chuck, gravy beef, osso buco and diced beef are all in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/). Every cut is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. Orders over $500 ship free.`],
    ],
  },
  {
    ...meta('beef-skirt'),
    title: 'Beef Skirt: What It Is, How to Cook It and What to Buy Instead',
    seoTitle: 'Beef Skirt Guide: Best Ways to Cook It',
    excerpt: `Beef skirt explained: where the cut comes from, how it differs from flank and hanger, how to cook and slice it, and the closest halal cuts we deliver.`,
    body: [
      ['p', `Beef skirt is one of the most flavourful cuts on the animal, and one of the most misunderstood. It is long, thin and coarse-grained, it cooks in minutes, and when it is handled properly it is tender, juicy and deeply beefy. Cooked carelessly, it can be chewy. This guide explains where skirt comes from, how it compares with flank, hanger and bavette, the best ways to cook it, and which cuts we deliver that do the same job.`],
      ['h2', `Where beef skirt comes from`],
      ['p', `Skirt comes from the plate, the area below the ribs. There are two skirt muscles. The outside skirt is part of the diaphragm; it is the prized one, with a rich, almost mineral flavour and loose grain. The inside skirt is a separate muscle from the flank side of the plate, a little thicker and slightly chewier. Both are long, flat strips with very visible muscle fibres running across their width.`],
      ['p', `Skirt is called "arrachera" in Mexico, where it is the traditional cut for fajitas, and it features in Korean, Thai, Argentinian and French cooking. Its popularity with restaurants means it can be harder to find at retail than its flavour deserves.`],
      ['h2', `Beef skirt steak vs flank, hanger and bavette`],
      ['p', `A beef skirt steak is often confused with these neighbouring cuts. They share a coarse grain and a love of high heat, but they are different muscles:`],
      ['ul', [
        `[Flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/): from the belly, behind the plate. Thicker, leaner and more even than skirt, with a similar coarse grain.`,
        `[Hanger steak](/product/beef-hanger-steak/) (onglet): hangs from the diaphragm near the kidneys. Very tender and rich, with a central sinew.`,
        `Bavette: in French butchery, the flank or the sirloin flap. Our [chuck tail flap](/product/beef-chuck-tail-flap/) is a well-marbled, loose-grained cut that cooks in a similar way.`,
      ]],
      ['p', `We do not list skirt as a separate product at the moment, so if a recipe calls for it, flank and hanger are the closest substitutes in our range, and both are excellent in their own right.`],
      ['h2', `How to cook skirt beef`],
      ['p', `Skirt beef rewards two approaches: very fast and very hot, or long and slow. Anything in between tends to toughen it.`],
      ['h3', `Hot and fast`],
      ['p', `Marinate or simply season the steak, then grill, barbecue or pan-sear it over the highest heat you can manage. Skirt is thin, so 2 to 3 minutes a side usually gets you to medium-rare. Pull it off at about 52 to 55°C on an instant-read thermometer and rest it for 5 to 8 minutes.`],
      ['h3', `Low and slow`],
      ['p', `Skirt also braises well. Roll it around a filling and simmer it in a sauce, or cut it into chunks for stews and curries. Its loose grain soaks up sauce, and an hour or two of gentle cooking softens it nicely.`],
      ['h2', `Slicing across the grain`],
      ['p', `This is the single most important thing to know about skirt. Its fibres run across the width of the strip, so cut the long strip into pieces about 15 cm long first, then turn each piece and slice it thinly across those fibres. Sliced with the grain, the same steak eats like rope; sliced against it, it is genuinely tender.`],
      ['h2', `Marinades that suit skirt`],
      ['ul', [
        `Lime, garlic, cumin and chilli for fajitas and tacos (see our [beef fajitas](/blog/beef-fajitas/) recipe)`,
        `Soy, garlic, sesame oil and brown sugar for Korean-style grilled beef`,
        `Olive oil, parsley, oregano, garlic and lemon juice for chimichurri`,
        `Fish sauce, lime and palm sugar for a [Thai beef salad](/blog/thai-beef-salad/)`,
      ]],
      ['p', `Thin cuts take on flavour fast, so 30 minutes to 4 hours of marinating is plenty. Very acidic marinades left overnight can make the surface mushy.`],
      ['h2', `Halal beef meat for fast grilling`],
      ['p', `If you are looking for halal beef meat with skirt's character, here is how our range lines up. [Flank steak](/product/flank-steak-beef/) is the best all-round substitute for fajitas, salads and stir-fries. [Hanger steak](/product/beef-hanger-steak/) is the choice for a rich, tender steak-frites style dinner. [Chuck tail flap](/product/beef-chuck-tail-flap/) suits marinades and the barbecue. For stir-fries, our ready-cut [beef stir-fry strips](/product/beef-stir-fry-strips/) save time.`],
      ['h2', `Beef rump steak: the easy alternative`],
      ['p', `If you want a steak that is simple to cook, widely loved and good value, a [beef rump steak](/product/rump-beef-steaks/) is hard to beat. It has a similar robust flavour, a firmer texture and is more forgiving of a slightly cooler grill. It slices well for salads and sandwiches too.`],
      ['h2', `Beef skirt questions`],
      ['h3', `Is beef skirt tough?`],
      ['p', `Not when it is cooked correctly. Skirt has long, coarse fibres that are tender when cooked quickly to medium-rare and sliced thinly across the grain, or when braised slowly. It becomes chewy when it is cooked to medium-well on a cool grill or sliced with the grain.`],
      ['h3', `What is skirt beef used for?`],
      ['p', `Fajitas and tacos, steak sandwiches, Korean barbecue, Thai and Vietnamese salads, stir-fries and slow-cooked dishes such as Cornish pasties and braised rolls. Its loose texture holds marinades and sauces particularly well.`],
      ['h3', `What temperature should I cook skirt to?`],
      ['p', `Medium-rare is ideal: about 55 to 57°C after resting. Skirt can be served medium (around 60°C), but beyond that it tightens and loses its juiciness quickly.`],
      ['h3', `Should I remove the membrane?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Outside skirt often comes with a thin, silvery membrane on one side. Slip a sharp knife under it and pull it away in strips before cooking, or it will shrink and curl the steak.`],
      ['h3', `How many people does one skirt feed?`],
      ['p', `A whole outside skirt usually weighs 600 g to 1 kg, enough for three to five people as a main or six to eight in tacos with plenty of fillings.`],
      ['h3', `Can I cook flank the same way?`],
      ['p', `Yes. Flank is slightly thicker, so give it an extra minute a side, but the same marinades, the same high heat and the same thin, across-the-grain slicing apply.`],
      ['h3', `Can I cook skirt from frozen?`],
      ['p', `It is best thawed first. Skirt is thin, so it thaws overnight in the fridge. Pat it completely dry before cooking, because surface moisture prevents the deep crust that makes this cut so good.`],
      ['h2', `Buy halal beef online`],
      ['p', `Every cut in our [halal beef range](/shop/halal-beef/) is certified halal by Halal Control Australia and delivered chilled from Greenacre, Sydney, to anywhere in Australia. Compare cuts and prices per kilo online, and read our [beef onglet steak](/blog/beef-onglet-steak/) and [beef bavette](/blog/beef-bavette/) guides for more on these flavour-packed butcher's cuts.`],
    ],
  },
]
